{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-863",
              "name": "PureDB IPv6 allow/deny IP rules are silently discarded, bypassing per-user deny lists",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PureDB IPv6 allow/deny IP rules are silently discarded, bypassing per-user deny lists"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "PureDB records are colon-separated and pure-pw writes per-user allow/deny IP patterns verbatim, while the daemon reads the fields positionally. An IPv6 address literal contains colons, so configuring one writes extra fields: the deny pattern is silently dropped and a client from the denied address still authenticates. The allow-list variant shifts a fragment into the time-restriction field, which denies every login and also drops the allow restriction."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "PureDB records are colon-separated and pure-pw writes per-user allow/deny IP patterns verbatim, while the daemon reads the fields positionally. An IPv6 address literal contains colons, so configuring one writes extra fields: the deny pattern is silently dropped and a client from the denied address still authenticates. The allow-list variant shifts a fragment into the time-restriction field, which denies every login and also drops the allow restriction.\n\nA per-user network restriction configured through the documented administrative interface is silently not enforced. Any client holding valid credentials for the account can authenticate from the address the administrator intended to deny - and, because the rule disappears rather than degrading, from any other address as well. For allow lists the account becomes unusable, denying the legitimate user while the address restriction is also lost. The 1.0.54 changelog states that IP access rules now support IPv6 patterns; that does not hold for IPv6 address literals.\n\nValidate the four IP-pattern fields when the record is written so a value containing the record separator is rejected with a clear error instead of corrupting the record, and make the daemon treat a record with more fields than the format defines as invalid so any corrupted or hand-edited entry fails authentication rather than dropping its restrictions. Longer term, add an escape encoding for the field separator to the PureDB record format (writer and reader together, with a version marker) so IPv6 literals can be stored and evaluated, and document hostname rules as the supported way to express IPv6 restrictions in the meantime. Operators that already configured IPv6 literals should re-check affected accounts with pure-pw show and migrate to hostname rules.",
                "markdown": "PureDB records are colon-separated and pure-pw writes per-user allow/deny IP patterns verbatim, while the daemon reads the fields positionally. An IPv6 address literal contains colons, so configuring one writes extra fields: the deny pattern is silently dropped and a client from the denied address still authenticates. The allow-list variant shifts a fragment into the time-restriction field, which denies every login and also drops the allow restriction.\n\nA per-user network restriction configured through the documented administrative interface is silently not enforced. Any client holding valid credentials for the account can authenticate from the address the administrator intended to deny - and, because the rule disappears rather than degrading, from any other address as well. For allow lists the account becomes unusable, denying the legitimate user while the address restriction is also lost. The 1.0.54 changelog states that IP access rules now support IPv6 patterns; that does not hold for IPv6 address literals.\n\nValidate the four IP-pattern fields when the record is written so a value containing the record separator is rejected with a clear error instead of corrupting the record, and make the daemon treat a record with more fields than the format defines as invalid so any corrupted or hand-edited entry fails authentication rather than dropping its restrictions. Longer term, add an escape encoding for the field separator to the PureDB record format (writer and reader together, with a version marker) so IPv6 literals can be stored and evaluated, and document hostname rules as the supported way to express IPv6 restrictions in the meantime. Operators that already configured IPv6 literals should re-check affected accounts with pure-pw show and migrate to hostname rules."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-863",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/863.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-457",
              "name": "Uninitialized stack memory disclosed in EPRT IPv6 error reply",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Uninitialized stack memory disclosed in EPRT IPv6 error reply"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The IPv6 branch of the EPRT parser builds a struct sockaddr_storage on the stack and fills only the address family and the 16 address bytes, leaving sin6_scope_id uninitialized. When the supplied address differs from the control connection's peer, the structure is passed to getnameinfo(NI_NUMERICHOST), which appends `%<sin6_scope_id>` to the formatted address and returns uninitialized stack memory to the client in the 500 reply. An authenticated client can read four bytes of process stack per request, and the value changes with the commands executed earlier in the session."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The IPv6 branch of the EPRT parser builds a struct sockaddr_storage on the stack and fills only the address family and the 16 address bytes, leaving sin6_scope_id uninitialized. When the supplied address differs from the control connection's peer, the structure is passed to getnameinfo(NI_NUMERICHOST), which appends `%<sin6_scope_id>` to the formatted address and returns uninitialized stack memory to the client in the 500 reply. An authenticated client can read four bytes of process stack per request, and the value changes with the commands executed earlier in the session.\n\nAn authenticated client can read four bytes of uninitialized process stack per request through the EPRT error reply, and can influence which stale values are disclosed by the commands it issues first. This is a memory-disclosure primitive that can expose residual process data (including address/layout fragments useful for defeating ASLR in a follow-on exploit); in the tested build the disclosed word contained no directly sensitive value.\n\nZero the sockaddr_storage before populating it, as the IPv4 EPRT and PORT paths already do, so no uninitialized fields can reach getnameinfo. More generally, initialize every stack sockaddr structure at declaration and consider passing an explicit length from a single construction helper so partially filled address structures cannot be formatted or compared.",
                "markdown": "The IPv6 branch of the EPRT parser builds a struct sockaddr_storage on the stack and fills only the address family and the 16 address bytes, leaving sin6_scope_id uninitialized. When the supplied address differs from the control connection's peer, the structure is passed to getnameinfo(NI_NUMERICHOST), which appends `%<sin6_scope_id>` to the formatted address and returns uninitialized stack memory to the client in the 500 reply. An authenticated client can read four bytes of process stack per request, and the value changes with the commands executed earlier in the session.\n\nAn authenticated client can read four bytes of uninitialized process stack per request through the EPRT error reply, and can influence which stale values are disclosed by the commands it issues first. This is a memory-disclosure primitive that can expose residual process data (including address/layout fragments useful for defeating ASLR in a follow-on exploit); in the tested build the disclosed word contained no directly sensitive value.\n\nZero the sockaddr_storage before populating it, as the IPv4 EPRT and PORT paths already do, so no uninitialized fields can reach getnameinfo. More generally, initialize every stack sockaddr structure at declaration and consider passing an explicit length from a single construction helper so partially filled address structures cannot be formatted or compared."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-457",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/457.html"
            },
