      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-863",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "PureDB IPv6 allow/deny IP rules are silently discarded, bypassing per-user deny lists\n\nPureDB records are colon-separated and pure-pw writes per-user allow/deny IP patterns verbatim, while the daemon reads the fields positionally. An IPv6 address literal contains colons, so configuring one writes extra fields: the deny pattern is silently dropped and a client from the denied address still authenticates. The allow-list variant shifts a fragment into the time-restriction field, which denies every login and also drops the allow restriction."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/log_puredb.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 436,
                  "endLine": 441
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Daemon: reject records that still have fields after the last defined column (fail closed)"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/pure-pw.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 633,
                  "endLine": 635
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "pure-pw: refuse to write an IP rule containing the field separator"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "src/pure-pw.c (record writer) -> src/log_puredb.c (record reader and rule evaluation)",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/log_puredb.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 436,
                        "endLine": 441
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if (time_restrictions_check(time_restrictions) != 0) {\n        return -1;\n    }\n    if (pure_strtok2(&cursor, *PW_LINE_SEP) != NULL) {\n        return -1;              /* malformed record: unexpected trailing fields */\n    }\n\n    return 0;\n}"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/pure-pw.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 633,
                        "endLine": 635
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if (fp2 == NULL) {\n        return -1;\n    }\n    if ((pwinfo->allow_local_ip != NULL &&\n         strchr(pwinfo->allow_local_ip, *PW_LINE_SEP) != NULL) ||\n        (pwinfo->deny_local_ip != NULL &&\n         strchr(pwinfo->deny_local_ip, *PW_LINE_SEP) != NULL) ||\n        (pwinfo->allow_client_ip != NULL &&\n         strchr(pwinfo->allow_client_ip, *PW_LINE_SEP) != NULL) ||\n        (pwinfo->deny_client_ip != NULL &&\n         strchr(pwinfo->deny_client_ip, *PW_LINE_SEP) != NULL)) {\n        fputs(\"IP access rules cannot contain the ':' field separator;\\n\"\n              \"use a hostname rule for IPv6 clients instead of an address \"\n              \"literal.\\n\", stderr);\n        return -1;\n    }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Validate the four IP-pattern fields when the record is written so a value containing the record separator is rejected with a clear error instead of corrupting the record, and make the daemon treat a record with more fields than the format defines as invalid so any corrupted or hand-edited entry fails authentication rather than dropping its restrictions. Longer term, add an escape encoding for the field separator to the PureDB record format (writer and reader together, with a version marker) so IPv6 literals can be stored and evaluated, and document hostname rules as the supported way to express IPv6 restrictions in the meantime. Operators that already configured IPv6 literals should re-check affected accounts with pure-pw show and migrate to hostname rules.",
                "markdown": "Validate the four IP-pattern fields when the record is written so a value containing the record separator is rejected with a clear error instead of corrupting the record, and make the daemon treat a record with more fields than the format defines as invalid so any corrupted or hand-edited entry fails authentication rather than dropping its restrictions. Longer term, add an escape encoding for the field separator to the PureDB record format (writer and reader together, with a version marker) so IPv6 literals can be stored and evaluated, and document hostname rules as the supported way to express IPv6 restrictions in the meantime. Operators that already configured IPv6 literals should re-check affected accounts with pure-pw show and migrate to hostname rules."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "7b86abebed3036e9dbd6dbe4b085e701c7a293bf6a379bd7b595b3b7a8e80445"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "3.1",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "63f358032d11b954535dc4d0303ced5076e13792072a9e3f7fd0119705598a95",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 3.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 10:20:01 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/source",
              "endpoint": "src/pure-pw.c (record writer) -> src/log_puredb.c (record reader and rule evaluation)",
              "cwe": "CWE-863",
              "impact": "A per-user network restriction configured through the documented administrative interface is silently not enforced. Any client holding valid credentials for the account can authenticate from the address the administrator intended to deny - and, because the rule disappears rather than degrading, from any other address as well. For allow lists the account becomes unusable, denying the legitimate user while the address restriction is also lost. The 1.0.54 changelog states that IP access rules now support IPv6 patterns; that does not hold for IPv6 address literals.",
              "technical_analysis": "A PureDB (virtual-user) record is one line of fields separated by PW_LINE_SEP (`:`). The daemon reads it positionally in pw_puredb_parseline(): position 13 is the allowed local IP, 14 denied local IP, 15 allowed client IP, 16 denied client IP and 17 the time restrictions. pure_strtok2() in src/utils.c performs a plain single-character split with no quoting or escaping, and the writer add_new_pw_line() in src/pure-pw.c prints each pattern with `%s` without validation:\\n\\n```c title=src/pure-pw.c startLineNumber=711 endLineNumber=731\\n    if (pwinfo->allow_local_ip != NULL) {\\n        fprintf(fp2, \"%s\", pwinfo->allow_local_ip);   /* allowed local ip */\\n    }\\n    ...\\n    if (pwinfo->deny_client_ip != NULL) {\\n        fprintf(fp2, \"%s\", pwinfo->deny_client_ip);   /* denied client ip */\\n    }\\n```\\n\\nWith `-R '::1/128'` the two colons of the literal add two separators, so the deny column at position 16 is empty and `1/128` is pushed to position 18, which the parser never reads. access_ip_check() therefore sees an empty deny list and returns success for every address; pure-pw show also reports an empty deny list, so the misconfiguration is invisible to the operator. With `-r '::1/128'` (allow list) the fragment lands in the time-restriction column, time_restrictions_check() cannot parse it and returns -1, so every login is denied while the allow restriction is also gone.\\n\\nThe IPv6 CIDR matcher in access_ip_match() (ipv6_cidr_match() plus inet_pton(AF_INET6, ...)) is correct but cannot be reached with an IPv6 literal through the product's only rule-writing interface, because such a literal can never occupy a single colon-separated field. IPv6 hostname rules contain no colon and do work.",
              "remediation_steps": "Validate the four IP-pattern fields when the record is written so a value containing the record separator is rejected with a clear error instead of corrupting the record, and make the daemon treat a record with more fields than the format defines as invalid so any corrupted or hand-edited entry fails authentication rather than dropping its restrictions. Longer term, add an escape encoding for the field separator to the PureDB record format (writer and reader together, with a version marker) so IPv6 literals can be stored and evaluated, and document hostname rules as the supported way to express IPv6 restrictions in the meantime. Operators that already configured IPv6 literals should re-check affected accounts with pure-pw show and migrate to hostname rules.",
              "counterevidence": "The IPv6 matcher itself is correct and reachable through hostname rules (verified: -R localhost blocks both 127.0.0.1 and ::1), so this is not a general failure of IPv6 support. IPv4 rules are enforced correctly in the same test (control: -R 127.0.0.1/32 denies 127.0.0.1), and a well-formed record with no rules authenticates normally, so the defect is specific to address literals surviving the positional record format. Exploitation requires a non-default configuration step (an IPv6 literal rule rather than a hostname or IPv4 rule) plus valid credentials for the restricted account, which is why attack complexity is rated High. No path was found by which an unauthenticated client benefits.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the accounts protected by such IPv6 rules are administrative or the rule is the only control restricting them, the demonstrated loss of confidentiality becomes High (base 5.9); if no deployment configures IPv6 address literals, the practical impact is limited to a silent configuration error.",
              "fix_verification": "Applied both changes to a copy of the tree and rebuilt: `make -j4` and `make check` both exit 0 with no new compiler warnings; the fix_before blocks were dumped with line numbers to confirm they match the file character-for-character.\\n\\nSecurity closure: re-ran the proof of concept against the patched daemon with the same corrupted database that previously allowed the login. The IPv6-denied user from `::1` now answers `530 Login authentication failed` (was `230 OK`); the allow-list user still answers `530`; the IPv4 deny control still answers `530`.\\n\\nBypass review: the daemon-side check is a single \"no fields remain after the last column\" test inside the one parse function used by every PureDB login, so it catches corruption introduced through any field (client IP, local IP, home, gecos) and any number of extra separators, not only the literal used in the proof of concept. Records that end before the IP columns still take the pre-existing early-return path, and well-formed 18-field records still authenticate (verified with a permissive user). Patched `pure-pw` rejects any of the four patterns containing `:` and writes nothing, while IPv4 and hostname patterns are written unchanged.\\n\\nPreserved behaviour: permissive logins from `127.0.0.1` and `::1` still succeed, IPv4 deny rules are still enforced, and a rejected write leaves the password file untouched.\\n\\nGaps: no exhaustive corpus of historical record shapes was executed (legacy short records were reasoned from the code plus the permissive-record test), and the complete escape-encoding fix was described but not implemented. Verification ran on a copy of the source tree; the same steps apply to the live tree.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Build the tree and use the in-tree `src/pure-pw` and `src/pure-ftpd`.\\n2. Create a virtual user whose client-IP deny list contains an IPv6 literal: `pure-pw useradd <login> -f <passwd> -u <uid> -g <gid> -d <home> -R '::1/128'`.\\n3. Create a control user with an IPv4 deny rule: `-R '127.0.0.1/32'`.\\n4. Inspect the generated password lines: the IPv6 record has extra fields (`...:::::1/128:`) and an empty deny column; the IPv4 record is well formed.\\n5. Build the database with `pure-pw mkdb <puredb> -f <passwd>` and start `pure-ftpd -S <port> -E -j -A -l puredb:<puredb>`.\\n6. Log in as the IPv6-denied user from the IPv6 loopback address: the server answers `230 OK`, so the deny rule is not enforced.\\n7. Log in as the IPv4-denied control user from the IPv4 loopback address: the server answers `530 Login authentication failed`, proving the deny mechanism itself works and only the IPv6 rule was dropped.\\n8. Log in as the user configured with `-r '::1/128'` (allow list): the server answers `530` because the shifted fragment became an unparsable time restriction.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-457",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Uninitialized stack memory disclosed in EPRT IPv6 error reply\n\nThe IPv6 branch of the EPRT parser builds a struct sockaddr_storage on the stack and fills only the address family and the 16 address bytes, leaving sin6_scope_id uninitialized. When the supplied address differs from the control connection's peer, the structure is passed to getnameinfo(NI_NUMERICHOST), which appends `%<sin6_scope_id>` to the formatted address and returns uninitialized stack memory to the client in the 500 reply. An authenticated client can read four bytes of process stack per request, and the value changes with the commands executed earlier in the session."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/ftpd.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 862,
                  "endLine": 864
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Zero the stack address structure before populating it (the IPv4 EPRT path already does this)"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "EPRT",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/ftpd.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 862,
                        "endLine": 864
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "memset(&a, 0, sizeof a);\n    if (generic_aton(deb, &a) != 0) {\n        goto nope;\n    }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Zero the sockaddr_storage before populating it, as the IPv4 EPRT and PORT paths already do, so no uninitialized fields can reach getnameinfo. More generally, initialize every stack sockaddr structure at declaration and consider passing an explicit length from a single construction helper so partially filled address structures cannot be formatted or compared.",
                "markdown": "Zero the sockaddr_storage before populating it, as the IPv4 EPRT and PORT paths already do, so no uninitialized fields can reach getnameinfo. More generally, initialize every stack sockaddr structure at declaration and consider passing an explicit length from a single construction helper so partially filled address structures cannot be formatted or compared."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "ea13bc3b88b1f9af4c88a884d926247834312d06b21ebb799c9ad80c7dc34563"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "09067bd376b61b4fe8b061d361fec5a244dac286b8d3496b4f76c7946097dc60",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0002",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 10:20:42 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/source",
              "endpoint": "EPRT",
              "cwe": "CWE-457",
              "impact": "An authenticated client can read four bytes of uninitialized process stack per request through the EPRT error reply, and can influence which stale values are disclosed by the commands it issues first. This is a memory-disclosure primitive that can expose residual process data (including address/layout fragments useful for defeating ASLR in a follow-on exploit); in the tested build the disclosed word contained no directly sensitive value.",
              "technical_analysis": "The IPv6 branch of the EPRT parser declares the address structure without initializing it:\\n\\n```c title=src/ftpd.c startLineNumber=838 endLineNumber=866\\nstatic void do_ipv6_port(char *p, char delim)\\n{\\n    char *deb;\\n    struct sockaddr_storage a;        /* uninitialized */\\n    ...\\n    if (generic_aton(deb, &a) != 0) {\\n        goto nope;\\n    }\\n    doport2(a, (unsigned int) port);\\n}\\n```\\n\\n`generic_aton()` only sets the family (`ss_family`) and the 16 address bytes (`sin6_addr`); `sin6_flowinfo` and `sin6_scope_id` are left with whatever the stack contains. `doport2()` then compares the address with the control connection peer and, when they differ and FXP is not allowed, formats both addresses with `getnameinfo(..., NI_NUMERICHOST)`. glibc's IPv6 formatter appends `%<sin6_scope_id>` whenever the scope id is non-zero, so the uninitialized word is rendered as a decimal suffix in the `500` reply to the client.\\n\\nThe IPv4 EPRT branch (`memset(&a, 0, sizeof a);` before filling) and `doport()` already do this correctly, which confirms the omission is specific to the IPv6 helper.",
              "remediation_steps": "Zero the sockaddr_storage before populating it, as the IPv4 EPRT and PORT paths already do, so no uninitialized fields can reach getnameinfo. More generally, initialize every stack sockaddr structure at declaration and consider passing an explicit length from a single construction helper so partially filled address structures cannot be formatted or compared.",
              "counterevidence": "The leak only occurs on the IPv6 EPRT error path when the supplied address differs from the control-connection peer (the normal FXP-rejection reply); when the address matches the peer no address is formatted and nothing leaks. In this build the disclosed word was constant for a given command sequence and contained no recognizable secret, so the demonstrated impact is limited to four bytes of process memory per request rather than a direct credential or pointer disclosure. The IPv4 EPRT and PORT paths zero the structure and are unaffected. Confirmed by running the same requests against a patched build, which returns a plain address.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Evidence that the disclosed words contain ASLR-relevant pointers or other sensitive process data in a production build would raise this finding; if the build always zeroes the field in practice (for example due to a zeroed stack), it would drop to informational.",
              "fix_verification": "Applied the one-line change to a copy of the tree and rebuilt (`make -j4`, `make check`; both exit 0, no new warnings). The fix_before block was dumped with line numbers to confirm it matches the file character-for-character.\\n\\nSecurity closure: the same requests that previously returned `2001:db8::1%3724148534`, `::1%3724148534` and `fe80::1%3724148534` now return `2001:db8::1`, `::1` and `fe80::1`, including after the stack-shaping sequence (`TYPE` with a 4000-byte argument) that previously changed the disclosed word. The uninitialized read is gone because the whole structure is zeroed before it is populated.\\n\\nBypass review: both callers of `generic_aton()` were inspected. The IPv4 EPRT branch and `doport()` already `memset` the structure, and the remaining caller (the operator-only `-t` trusted-IP option at startup) only feeds `addrcmp()`, which compares address bytes and never formats the structure; it is not reachable by a client. Within `do_ipv6_port` the structure is used only for `doport3()` protocol selection, `addrcmp()` and `getnameinfo()`, all of which now see a fully defined structure.\\n\\nPreserved behaviour: `EPRT |1|...` and `EPRT |2|...` still succeed for matching addresses, the FXP rejection reply still names both addresses, `PORT` and `PASV` are unchanged, and numeric formatting of normal addresses is identical.\\n\\nGaps: the check is behavioural (no MemorySanitizer run, since an MSan-instrumented build was not produced); verification ran on a copy of the source tree, and the same one-line change applies to the live tree.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Build the tree and start `pure-ftpd` locally with a virtual user (for example `pure-ftpd -S <port> -E -j -A -l puredb:<db>`).\\n2. Connect and authenticate (`USER`/`PASS`).\\n3. Send `EPRT |2|2001:db8::1|1025|` - an IPv6 address that differs from the peer address.\\n4. Observe the `500 I won't open a connection to 2001:db8::1%3724148534 (only to 127.0.0.1)` reply: the `%3724148534` suffix is the uninitialized `sin6_scope_id` (0xddf9ff36).\\n5. Repeat on a fresh connection without any prior command: the same word is returned. Send a long command first (for example `TYPE` followed by a 4000-byte argument) and repeat the EPRT: the disclosed word changes (0x82e29900), proving it is stale stack content that the client can shape.\\n6. Submit a very long EPRT and repeat the same correction, and the value can be sampled across a session; the fix removes the suffix entirely, returning the plain address.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/quota-accounting-bypass-fail-open-error-handling",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "kind": "open",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "quota accounting bypass / fail-open error handling — Requires further review: src/quotas.c + quota_update() callers in src/ftpd.c\n\nquota_update() zeroes *quota at quotas.c:97 before parsing, and returns -1 on a record without a space (quotas.c:116-121); both callers treat that error as \"no quota problem\" (ftpd.c:3702-3704 returns 0, ftpd.c:4303-4311 skips the whole check). Fail-open is certain at code level; the gap is that no remote way to corrupt /.ftpquota was found (checknamesanity blocks any name containing .ftpquota while quotas are active, ftpd.c:775-786)."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "src/quotas.c + quota_update() callers in src/ftpd.c"
                }
              ]
            }
