        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/jail-escape-path-overflow-from-ftp-path-arguments",
          "ruleIndex": 8,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "jail escape / path overflow from FTP path arguments — Ruled out: src/ftpd.c path handling (checknamesanity, docwd, get_atomic_file, STOR/DELE paths)\n\nDynamically re-verified on two live builds of this tree (real-chroot build and --with-virtualchroot + --with-diraliases build), PureDB user, ftplib/socket driver. No path argument reached outside the jail root: RETR/CWD/LIST/NLST/STAT/MLSD/SIZE/MDTM/MKD/RMD/DELE/RNFR+RNTO/SITE CHMOD/SITE UTIME with `../../..`, absolute `/etc/passwd`, 1000x `../`, `.//..//..`, `a/..//..//..`, trailing separators, `..%2f` and backslash variants all returned 550/553 or resolved inside the jail; 4096-byte names fail with ENAMETOOLONG and never truncate into a different existing path. Named controls: kernel chroot() in the default build; fakexlate() prefix test `strncmp(curdirtmp, chroot_base, chroot_len) != 0 || curdirtmp[chroot_len] != '/'` plus the EPERM `..` checks in virtual-chroot builds; checknamesanity() for control chars/backslash. dot_ok==1 for chrooted users is therefore compensated by those two controls on every attacker-reachable path, and the `..` components it allows are normalized by the kernel (retested with `inside/../file1.txt` which succeeds only inside the jail)."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "src/ftpd.c path handling (checknamesanity, docwd, get_atomic_file, STOR/DELE paths)"
                }
