        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/jail-escape-via-virtual-chroot-path-rewriting",
          "ruleIndex": 10,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "jail escape via virtual-chroot path rewriting — Ruled out: src/fakechroot.c fakexlate()/fakesymlink()\n\nResolved by building --with-virtualchroot and exercising it live. fakexlate() cannot be walked out of: `..` at the root is clamped (`../..` repeatedly keeps cwd at `/`), `a/../x` is rejected with EPERM, absolute arguments are re-based on chroot_base, and a sibling directory whose path shares the jail prefix (`.../ftproot_sibling` vs jail `.../ftproot`) was not reachable by any command. fakegetcwd()'s prefix-only strncmp cannot see a foreign directory because curdir is only ever assigned from fakexlate() output, which always begins with chroot_base + '/'. The flagged fakesymlink() is unreachable: no FTP command creates symlinks (only link() in tryautorename(), src/ftpd.c:3591/3610). The one escape observed — a pre-existing symlink in the home directory pointing outside the jail (`abs_link -> /etc/passwd`, `up_link -> .../outside`) is read through RETR in virtual-chroot mode — is the documented purpose of the option (README:253-260 \"symbolic links are always followed... This feature isn't enabled by default\"); the same requests fail in the default chroot build. Not a defect."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
