          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "memory corruption / DoS via glob expansion of FTP path arguments — No issue identified: src/bsd-glob.c sglob()/glob0()/globexp2() with client-supplied patterns (LIST, STAT, NLST)\n\nFuzzed live with 18 pathological patterns per command (thousands of `*`, unbalanced { } [ ], `[a-` ranges, 4000-char patterns, 500-char backslash runs) plus 1000-component `../` paths: every request returned a normal 226/550 reply, no crash, no session or daemon death, no memory errors observed. Structurally: glob_() rejects patterns >= PATH_MAX (bsd-glob.c:194) and GLOB_LIMIT caps stars at 3 via check_stars() (:158-161); callers pass max_ls_files/max_ls_depth. Matched names are only stat()ed and listed, so globbing cannot widen filesystem reach: path arguments still go through the chroot/fakexlate translation for the chdir()/stat() that follow."
