        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/integer-overflow-in-record-length-offset-handling-producing-a-db-the-reader-over-reads",
          "ruleIndex": 41,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Integer overflow in record length/offset handling producing a DB the reader over-reads — Ruled out: puredb/src/puredb_write.c (+ puredb_read.c consumer)\n\nNamed controls: puredbw_add validates key_len/content_len against the u32 boundary and projects data_offset_counter + offset_first_data + the worst-case 256*4 writekeys bump before storing anything (puredb_write.c:93-125), returning -1 rather than writing a wrapped offset; writekeys computes offset_first_data to its final value in the offset-table pass before any data offset is stored (203-238); the reader bounds every access (read_be_long puredb_read.c:53, read_memcmp puredb_read.c:83, table/pointer/key/content checks at 175-196, 245-268, 273-303). Verified dynamically: pure-pw mkdb on a file with a 4094-char gecos produced a 6339-byte DB, and an ASan/UBSan reader opened it and read the 2042-byte and 58-byte records with no OOB or UB. Header/length values above 4 GiB are unreachable from pure-pw anyway because each record comes from a LINE_MAX=4096 fgets buffer."
          },
