        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/privileged-parsing-of-attacker-planted-scoreboard-files-oob-read-format-string-toctou",
          "ruleIndex": 45,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Privileged parsing of attacker-planted scoreboard files (OOB read, format string, TOCTOU) — Ruled out: src/pure-ftpwho.c + src/ftpwho-update.c + src/ftpwho-read.c\n\nNamed controls on the root-parsing path: the scoreboard directory must be root-owned and is forced to 0700 (ftpwho-update.c:108-130), entries are opened O_NOFOLLOW with 0600 and must be regular, root-owned and exactly sizeof(FTPWhoEntry) (pure-ftpwho.c:826-841), account/filename are always NUL-terminated by the daemon on all four write sites (ftpd.c:1461-1465, 1938-1942, 3372-3380, 4335-4343), and ftpwho_read_count NUL-terminates its 128-byte read (ftpwho-read.c:73). Tested first-hand as root against /var/run/pure-ftpd: a symlink to /etc/shadow, a 100-byte file, and a file owned by uid 1000 were all skipped; a 1456-byte crafted entry with no NUL in account/filename printed garbage out of the zero-filled mapping but did not crash (mmap page padding bounds the over-read). Placement requires root-owned 0600 files in a root-only dir, so no FTP user (uid-ranged) can plant one. Minor hardening notes, not findings: `(st.st_mode & 0600) != 0600` accepts 0644 scoreboards, and pure-ftpwho does not itself validate SCOREBOARD_PATH (it chdir()s blindly) — harmless only because the daemon created it root-owned 0700."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "src/pure-ftpwho.c + src/ftpwho-update.c + src/ftpwho-read.c"
                }
