        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/oob-read-connection-count-bypass-in-the-proc-net-tcp-parser",
          "ruleIndex": 47,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "OOB read / connection-count bypass in the /proc/net/tcp parser — Ruled out: src/daemons.c\n\nInput is kernel-generated /proc/net/tcp[6] (/proc is root-owned and not writable by FTP users), and the parser cannot write out of bounds: reads are capped at 2048-e with buf[2048] permanently 0 (daemons.c:25-33, 126-134). The only defect is a one-byte over-read: if a 2048-byte chunk ends without a ':' the cursor can become e+1 and strtoul(buf+b) reads buf[2049] (daemons.c:52-54 and 148-151). That needs a line of >=2048 bytes with no field separator — the kernel's fixed-format rows are far shorter — so it is not reachable with attacker-influenced data and produces a miscount at worst, not memory corruption. Not filed."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "src/daemons.c"
                }
