        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/memory-safety-in-fact-listing-formatting-long-names-symlink-targets-long-path-arguments",
          "ruleIndex": 52,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Memory safety in fact/listing formatting (long names, symlink targets, long path arguments) — No issue identified: MLSD/MLST/LIST/NLST output path (src/ls.c domlsd/donlst/listfile/dolist, src/ftpd.c modernformat/domlst)\n\nEvery formatted line goes through snprintf into a PATH_MAX-sized buffer and the return value is checked with SNCHECK, which triggers a clean _EXIT instead of an overflow; readlink() into link_target is bounded by sizeof-1 and NUL-terminated, and domlsd skips entries whose name cannot be formatted. Verified under the ASAN build against a directory containing a 255-byte filename, 100/1000/4000/4090-byte symlink targets, high-bit UTF-8 names and control-character names, via MLSD, MLST, LIST -la, LIST -R, NLST and MLST with a 4000-byte path: no ASAN reports. (Observation only, not a security issue: the non-guest branch of modernformat omits the symlink-target %s that the guest branch prints, so symlink targets are not shown to virtual users.)"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
